\section{Arguments and outputs}{

This facade knows nothing about any individual routine -- not its arguments,
not how many values it returns. See the FSDA documentation for a routine's
own signature.

A 1-D input crosses as a MATLAB \strong{row}, so pass \code{y} as an \verb{(n, 1)} matrix
when a routine wants a column. Outputs come back in MATLAB's natural shape,
and indices returned from MATLAB stay 1-based.

FSDA routines return a struct, which arrives as a named list, so \code{out$beta}
works with no wrapper. MATLAB's own functions often return several unnamed
positional outputs instead; request them with \code{nargout} and unpack by
position:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{raw = fsda$kmeans(Y, 3, Replicates = 10, nargout = 4)
idx = raw[[1]]
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Plotting routines}{

Pass \code{nargout = 0} for a routine called to draw a figure, so no graphics
handle is requested:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{fsda$yXplot(y, X, nargout = 0)
render_figures()
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{boxplotb} is the exception worth knowing: it draws \emph{and} returns a real data
struct, so it keeps \code{nargout = 1}.
}

\section{Struct arguments}{

The engine marshals MATLAB structs out but not in, so a routine taking a
struct argument is driven by keeping the struct in the workspace:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{eval_m(NULL, "outEDA = FSMeda(Y, bsb, 'plots', 0);", nargout = 0)
eval_m(NULL, "malfwdplot(outEDA);", nargout = 0)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Typos}{

The first time a name is used, MATLAB is asked whether the routine exists, so
a misspelling fails in R before anything is marshalled.

On Windows this check resolves through a case-insensitive filesystem, so a
name differing from a real routine only by case (\code{FSRR} against FSDA's
\code{FSRr}) passes the check and is then rejected by MATLAB's case-sensitive
dispatch. The error still arrives, just from MATLAB rather than from R.

\code{options(fsdabridge.validate = FALSE)} skips the check entirely.
}

